# Reverse proxy / TLS termination for a production BBBFFL deployment
# (issue #243). Copy to deploy/production/Caddyfile and set BBBFFL_DOMAIN in
# bbbffl_app/.env.production -- Caddy substitutes {$BBBFFL_DOMAIN} from the
# container's environment at startup (env_file: in compose.production.yaml).
#
# What this does, and why it satisfies issue #243's proxy requirements:
#   - forwards to the app service on the compose-internal network only --
#     app/database are never published to the host (see
#     compose.production.yaml); only this proxy publishes 80/443.
#   - Caddy's reverse_proxy sets X-Forwarded-For/-Proto/-Host and preserves
#     the original Host header by default -- no extra header_up directives
#     are needed for the app to see the real client IP/scheme, and nothing
#     here strips or rewrites the Authorization/X-Admin-Token headers
#     BBBFFL's own authorization boundary (app/authorization.py,
#     app/routes/admin.py) depends on.
#   - HTTPS is explicit: Caddy provisions/renews a Let's Encrypt certificate
#     for BBBFFL_DOMAIN automatically and redirects plain HTTP to HTTPS --
#     there is no separate certbot/manual-renewal step to maintain.
#   - PostgreSQL is never reverse-proxied or exposed here at all -- see
#     "does not expose PostgreSQL publicly" in docs/production-operations.md.
#
# What remains environment-specific (cannot be provided generically here):
#   - BBBFFL_DOMAIN must be a real hostname that already resolves (DNS A/AAAA
#     record) to this host's public IP *before* first start, so Let's
#     Encrypt's HTTP-01 challenge can succeed. A domain that does not yet
#     resolve will fail certificate issuance -- this is inherent to ACME,
#     not a BBBFFL or Caddy limitation.
#   - Port 80 and 443 must be reachable from the public internet on this
#     host (router port-forwarding for a home-server deployment).

{$BBBFFL_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy app:8000
}

# Rehearsal/staging alternative with no public DNS or open ports: replace
# the site block above with the one below to get a self-signed certificate
# Caddy trusts locally, so the full HTTPS path (including the app's own
# BBBFFL_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://... expectation) can still be rehearsed on
# a private host or a disposable container. Never use `tls internal` for
# the real production domain -- browsers/other devices will not trust it.
#
# localhost {
# 	tls internal
# 	reverse_proxy app:8000
# }
